## THE 2.31x WAS A SELF-SPEEDUP, AND THIS IS WHAT IT WAS WORTH

The sweep's 2.31x was the SOLVE alone (4.542 -> 1.963 ms). This cell measures ONE factorization
plus SIXTEEN solves, and min-degree moved the factor 14.502 -> 35.364 ms because the exact
minimum-degree ordering is O(V^2) and `splu` pays it inside every factor call. Whole job
19.044 -> 37.327 ms, 1.96x worse — and the live row lands at 0.344404, within 2% of the ~0.35
predicted from the sweep before it was run.

So "0.684 x 2.31 = 1.58, a win" does not follow, and this row is the evidence. A solve-only
speedup multiplied against a whole-job ratio is a category error; the factor is 88% of the job
under this ordering.

## WHAT THE FILL RESULT STILL SAYS, and it is not nothing

Fill really is 2.61x smaller (357,568 -> 137,078) and 1.58x below SuperLU's 216,528, and the
solve really is 2.31x faster. Everything downstream of the ordering improves. The entire loss
is the ordering's OWN cost.

WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED, and I am not repeating my earlier projection as if it were: I estimated
an ~8.1 ms job for a hypothetical 1 ms AMD by assuming numeric factor time scales linearly with
fill. This run cannot confirm that, because it does not separate min-degree's ordering cost from
its numeric factor cost — it only gives their sum, 35.364 ms. Until that split is measured the
AMD upside is unquantified, and the honest bound is only "somewhere between no gain and the
solve's 2.31x".

THE NEXT MEASUREMENT, precisely: time `sparse_lu_fill_ordering` alone under RCM and under
min-degree on this fixture. That yields the ordering cost directly, and therefore both the
numeric-factor-vs-fill scaling and the real AMD ceiling. It is a small probe and it decides
whether an AMD implementation is worth a session.

